Press @tofu{1 block}(extra firm) with paper towels to remove some of the water. Grate tofu on a box grater, on medium/large holes.

Heat @olive oil{1%tablespoon} in a #nonstick skillet{} over medium-high heat. Add the tofu shreds; sauté until golden and lightly crisped. It usually takes about ~{10-15%minutes} to really get it to have some crispy/chewy texture.

Add @teriyaki{1/4 cup} and @garlic{1 clove}(grated) to the “thirsty” tofu. Sauté for another ~{5-10%minutes} to caramelize further. Add 1-2 tablespoons more sauce as desired. Transfer to a separate bowl and wipe out the pan.

In the same pan, heat @red curry paste{1%tablespoon}, @white miso{1%tablespoon}, @peanut butter{1-2%tablespoons}, @soy sauce{2%tablespoons}, and @brown sugar{1%tablespoon} until it’s worked into a thick paste. Add @coconut milk{1 (14-ounce) can} and @water{1 1/2 cups} + @Better Than Bouillon{1 1/2 teaspoons}(roasted chicken or vegetable base), and whisk until incorporated. Simmer for ~{5-10%minutes} until slightly thickened and creamy.

Meanwhile, boil @ramen noodles{6 ounces}(uncooked) in a separate pot for ~{3-4%minutes}; drain, and add to sauce. Simmer for ~{1-2%minutes} until the noodles are well-coated and add more broth if you want it to be more soupy.

Transfer noodles into bowls, and pour a scoop of the broth over the top. Top with the tofu, @cucumbers{}(finely sliced), @cilantro and/or @green onion{?}, and @chili crisp{}. 

